# Runbook

Local: `vecten up` starts the stack, `vecten down` stops it. Logs: `docker compose --project-directory .vecten/stack --env-file .env -f .vecten/stack/docker-compose.yml logs`.
Cloud sections are added by `vecten init --deploy`.
[% if 'platform' in components %]
## Connectors

Each source lives under `connectors/<source>/` with `connector.py` exposing `source()`. Credentials:
`vecten secret set <NAME>`, read in code with `secret("<NAME>")`; the stack passes every secret the
declaration names into the Airflow containers on the next `vecten up`. Run locally: `vecten connector run
<source> [--object <name>]`; record fixtures: `vecten connector run <source> --record`; inspect:
`vecten raw schema <source> --json`. Each object syncs through its own dlt pipeline, so a failing object
neither blocks nor resets the others; the sync DAGs are `orchestration/dags/sync_<source>_<group>.py`.
A connector's Python dependencies are installed in the Airflow image from `vecten.toml`; install them
where `vecten` runs as well, for example `uv tool install vecten --with <package>`. Failures post to
`ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL` when that secret is set, each with its issue id. `vecten issues` (the laptop) or
`vecten issues --env <env>` lists every failure that has not run green since, `vecten issues show
<id>` prints its redacted log excerpt, and `skills/operate/SKILL.md` works through the list.

`--record` reloads the object from the start so the fixture is complete; run it on the first sync or
whenever the fixture should be refreshed. It writes `connectors/<source>/fixtures/<object>.jsonl` only
for objects that synced and yielded records; the test `connectors/<source>/test_<source>.py` replays
them into DuckDB with the network blocked and is skipped until fixtures exist. That test and
`vecten dbt-build-local` load the fixtures straight into the raw tables and never run `connector.py`,
so a code change that breaks every live sync still passes CI while the fixtures are unchanged; after
changing `connector.py`, `vecten connector run <source> --json` is the live proof. Fixtures hold real
records from the source (up to 200 per object) and are committed to the project, so check them for
personal data before the repository is shared.

After changing which secrets a connector reads, update the `secrets` list of its declaration in
`vecten.toml` and run `vecten up` again.

Known limits:

- A dlt verified source that exposes only `@dlt.resource` functions and no `@dlt.source` is refused by
  `vecten connector add`; start it from a generic scaffold (`--from rest`, `--from sql` or
  `--from filesystem`) instead.
- The wrapper passes a verified source's `dlt.secrets.value` parameters as `secret("<SOURCE>_<PARAM>")`
  but does not wire its `dlt.config.value` parameters; pass those in `connector.py`.
- Editing a generic scaffold's `connector.py` re-declares the source as `source = "custom"` on its next
  `vecten connector run`.
[% endif %][% if 'platform' in components %]
## dbt

The DAG `dbt_build` (`orchestration/dags/dbt_build.py`) builds the dbt project once every sync
asset `raw_<source>.<group>` has updated since its last run; with no connectors it runs only when
triggered. Its tasks: `deps` resolves the dbt packages, then `build` and `freshness` run side by
side, then `convention` runs the convention tests against what `build` produced, then
`mark_built`. A failed task retries once after ten minutes and posts to `ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL` when
that secret is set.

Each run's results are archived under its run id, so a failed run's results outlive the next run:

- `dbt/runs/<run_id>/build/run_results.json`
- `dbt/runs/<run_id>/convention/run_results.json`
- `dbt/runs/<run_id>/freshness/sources.json`

On the laptop stack they land in the project's `.vecten/artifacts`, which the Airflow containers
mount at `/opt/airflow/artifacts`, the default root that `VECTEN_ARTIFACT_DIR` moves when set;
for example `ls .vecten/artifacts/dbt/runs`. On a deployed VM they land in the deployment's
bucket under `platform/` with the same keys. `vecten issues` reads them from either place and
lists each failing test, model and stale source; see `docs/operations.md` in the Vecten
repository.

`mark_built` writes the commit of the shipped bundle to `dbt/built_commit` after a green full
build and convention run. It reads the commit from `VECTEN_BUNDLE_COMMIT`, which only a shipped
bundle sets, so on the laptop stack it writes nothing. A run triggered with `select` or `exclude`
skips it, because the MCP serves a newly shipped semantic model only once the whole bundle is
built; after such a run, trigger `dbt_build` with no parameters to let the MCP switch.

`freshness` runs `dbt source freshness` on the sources `vecten connector add` declares in
`dbt/models/staging/sources.yml`, timed by dlt's load id: a source last loaded more than 36 hours
ago is `warn`, more than 72 hours ago `error`. The task logs each stale source with its status and
succeeds once `sources.json` is archived; it fails only when dbt could not write that file.
Freshness does not gate the build, because stale sources are a likely cause of a red build and
their record must exist either way.

The DAG keeps dbt's build output and installed packages in `/tmp/vecten-dbt` inside the Airflow
container (`DBT_TARGET_PATH`, `DBT_PACKAGES_INSTALL_PATH`), and `freshness` writes to its own
target there, so on the laptop stack the container never shares `dbt/target` or
`dbt/dbt_packages` with the host. The laptop stack and the host each resolve dbt dependencies
before every run and keep separate build directories.

The project's own dbt packages, from dbt hub or a public git repository, are listed in
`dbt/project-packages.yml`. Each hub package is pinned to one release (`version: 1.3.0`), and
each git package to a full commit id (`revision:`). The file is committed and shipped, and
every resolution appends its entries to the generated `dbt/packages.yml`. An unpinned, private,
local or duplicate entry stops the run with exit 6. On a deployed host the `deps` task installs
them on every run, which needs outbound HTTPS to `hub.getdbt.com`, `github.com` and
`codeload.github.com`. The hosts' security groups allow all outbound traffic. When those sites
are unreachable, `deps` fails and the build does not run.

On a laptop, `vecten dbt-build-local` runs the same build and convention steps on a disposable
warehouse from the connectors' recorded fixtures; `vecten dbt <args>` runs dbt against the stack's
Postgres[% if warehouse == 'snowflake' %], which a Snowflake project does not use, so here it
refuses without `--env`[% endif %].
[% if warehouse == 'snowflake' and render_deploy %]
`vecten dbt --env <env> <args>` runs dbt against a deployed environment's Snowflake warehouse as
its `LOADER` user, with `SNOWFLAKE_LOADER_PRIVATE_KEY` read from that environment's secrets
store. The key reaches dbt only through its process environment. On `prod` only `clean`,
`compile`, `debug`, `deps`, `docs generate`, `docs serve`, `ls`, `parse`, `show`,
`source freshness` and `test` run, and every other command exits 4. On `prod`, `test` also
leaves out unit tests and refuses `--store-failures`. `test` and `source freshness` are refused
while the project or a package declares an `on-run-start` or `on-run-end` hook, and `test` while
a test stores its failures. `LOADER` can write, so these checks guard against accidents. They
cannot catch SQL that Jinja runs at compile time. Every command runs on any other environment,
which is how to build a scratch Snowflake database: deploy an environment for it in its own
Snowflake account.
[% elif render_deploy %]
`vecten dbt --env <env>` exits 4 on this project: a deployed Postgres warehouse sits on the
environment's private network, which the laptop does not reach. The `dbt_build` DAG builds it,
and `vecten issues --env <env>` reports its failures.
[% endif %][% endif %][% if render_deploy and cloud == 'aws' %]
## AWS deployment

An environment such as `prod` runs in one AWS account: an EC2 virtual machine (VM) per host,
RDS Postgres instances in private subnets, a Route 53 record per component (`airflow.<domain>`
for the platform, `mcp.<domain>` for the MCP), and an S3 bucket for bundles, dbt artifacts and
Airflow logs. Under the `standard` profile the hosts are `platform` and `mcp`, one per component
the project has; under `minimal` one host, `main`, runs everything. The RDS instances are
`state`, which holds the `airflow` and `mcp_state` databases, and, when the platform builds a
Postgres warehouse, `warehouse`; under `minimal` the `state` instance holds all three databases
and then takes the warehouse class and storage of the tier `size` selects. After a deploy,
`.vecten/environments/<env>.json` holds the environment's Terraform outputs: each host's
instance id and address, the setup host, each instance's address and master secret, and the
bucket.

GitHub Actions does the deploying. `vecten deploy --env <env>` on the laptop dispatches the
`terraform-apply` workflow, which runs Terraform and then ships every host; every push to the
default branch runs the `ship` workflow, which ships the content without Terraform; a pull
request that changes `deploy/` or `vecten.toml` runs the `terraform-plan` workflow and gets the
plan as a comment. No VM accepts SSH: `vecten ship` reaches each VM through AWS Systems Manager
(SSM) Run Command.

The pull-request plan covers `prod`. The ship on every push covers the first environment of
the promotion order, `prod` when `vecten.toml` declares none, and each later environment of the
order ships only through `vecten promote --env <env>` ("Promotion order" below). Any other
environment is deployed and shipped when you dispatch it with `vecten deploy --env <env>` or
`vecten ship --env <env>`. Each ship recreates the stack's
containers on the host, so Airflow tasks running at that moment restart. A ship that fails
on the host prints, after its error, the last lines of `docker compose`'s own output (a failed
pull, build or port) and of each failing container's log, with secrets masked; on a VM without
a shell, that is where a failed start shows its cause.

In the commands below, `<prefix>` is `vecten-<project>-<env>`, the project's and the
environment's names lowercased with each run of other characters as one `-`, the project part
shortened when the two names are long. `aws rds describe-db-instances` lists the instances by
that prefix.

### What `vecten bootstrap` creates

`vecten bootstrap --env <env>` runs once per environment, from the laptop, with your own AWS
login (`aws sso login`). Running it again is safe. It creates or updates:

- The Terraform state bucket `vecten-<project>-<env>-state-<account id>`: private, versioned,
  encrypted and reachable over TLS only. `vecten destroy` keeps it.
- The GitHub Actions OIDC provider. OIDC lets a GitHub job exchange a short-lived token GitHub
  issues for temporary AWS credentials, so no AWS key is stored in GitHub.
- The permissions boundary `<prefix>-boundary`, the IAM policy that caps every host role: this
  environment's `platform` and `mcp` secrets, the RDS master secrets, the artifacts bucket, the
  SSM agent and CloudWatch Logs, and no IAM.
- The deploy role `<prefix>-deploy`, which the apply and ship jobs assume. It holds AWS's
  managed PowerUserAccess policy, which leaves out IAM, plus IAM actions limited to this
  environment's host role and instance profile names and to roles that carry the boundary. Only
  a job in this repository that declares `environment: <env>` may assume it.
- The plan role `<prefix>-plan`, which pull-request plans and plans on pushes to the default
  branch assume. It reads only what a Terraform plan refreshes, writes only Terraform's lock
  objects in the state bucket, and reads no secret.
- On GitHub: the environment `<env>` with the variables `VECTEN_AWS_ROLE_ARN`,
  `VECTEN_AWS_REGION` and `VECTEN_STATE_BUCKET`, and the repository variable
  `VECTEN_AWS_PLAN_ROLE_ARN_<ENV>`, the environment's name uppercased with `-` as `_`.

Bootstrap in a member account of an AWS Organization, never in the organization's management
account: PowerUserAccess includes `sso:*` and `identitystore:*`, which in the management account
administer IAM Identity Center, the sign-in for every account of the organization. The deploy
role may also rewrite the trust policy of this environment's host roles
(`iam:UpdateAssumeRolePolicy`), and IAM offers no condition on a trust policy's content; the
boundary still caps what any such role can do.

### Protect the deploy environment

The GitHub environment `<env>` is the deploy role's only gate: any workflow job in this
repository that declares `environment: <env>` can assume the role and change the AWS account.
When `vecten bootstrap` creates the environment, it limits deployments to the default branch.
GitHub offers that rule, a deployment branch policy, on public repositories and on paid plans.
On a private repository whose plan lacks it, bootstrap leaves the environment unrestricted and
prints a warning naming this section; anyone who can push a branch can then run a job that
deploys. Protect the environment by hand under the repository's Settings, Environments, `<env>`:
add required reviewers where the plan offers them, or move to a plan that offers branch
policies and limit the environment to the default branch. Bootstrap never rewrites an existing
environment, so rules added by hand survive a rerun; when an environment it finds has no
deployment branch restriction, it prints the same warning and leaves it as it is.

### Secrets

Every secret has an environment-variable-style name, and its prefix picks the scope it is
stored under in AWS Secrets Manager, as `vecten/[[ project_name ]]/<env>/<scope>/<NAME>`:

| Name | Scope | Read by a VM |
|---|---|---|
| `MCP_*` | `mcp` | the MCP VM |
| `DEPLOY_*` | `deploy` | none: credentials CI deploys with |
| `ADMIN_*` | `admin` | none: passwords from `vecten user add-admin` |
| any other name | `platform` | the platform VM |

Under `minimal` the one VM reads both `platform` and `mcp`. The deploy role reads and writes
all four scopes, as does an administrator's own AWS login. `vecten secret set <NAME> --env
<env>` stores a secret from a hidden prompt or standard input, `--from-local` copies it from
`.env`, and `vecten secret list --env <env>` prints names only.

The setup host is the one VM that reads the RDS master credentials: the MCP VM when the project
has the `mcp` component, else the platform VM, and `main` under `minimal`. On every ship it runs
the setup SQL on each instance, which creates the databases, their owner roles and the service
roles and is safe to repeat, and it generates the database passwords the stack uses:
`AIRFLOW_DB_PASSWORD` and `LOADER_PASSWORD` in the `platform` scope, `MCP_STATE_DB_PASSWORD`,
`MCP_PROVISIONER_PASSWORD` and `MCP_IMPERSONATOR_PASSWORD` in the `mcp` scope, each only when the
project has what it serves. Its role may create, write and read back exactly those names, so
that a replaced instance gets the same passwords again. The platform VM generates the Airflow
application secrets (`AIRFLOW_FERNET_KEY`, `AIRFLOW_API_SECRET_KEY`, `AIRFLOW_JWT_SECRET`,
`AIRFLOW_ADMIN_PASSWORD`, `AIRFLOW_BASIC_AUTH_PASSWORD`) on its first ship; its role may create
them but never write a new value, so DAG code cannot rewrite them. A ship stops naming any of
them that exists without a value; delete that secret without recovery and ship again. When an
MCP VM exists, the platform VM, which runs DAG code, holds no master credential.

Containers receive their secrets as environment variables. Docker keeps each container's
environment in `/var/lib/docker/containers/<id>/config.v2.json`, readable by root only, on the
VM's encrypted root volume. The two files the stack needs with secret content, the Airflow
passwords file and a basic-auth file that only an older release's NGINX reads, kept so a
rollback to one finds it, live under `/run/vecten/`, a memory-backed directory (tmpfs) with
mode 0600 that the VM agent rebuilds on every ship and at boot. vecten writes no other file
holding a secret on a VM.

Airflow's own login guards `https://airflow.[[ domain ]]`: user `admin`, password
`AIRFLOW_ADMIN_PASSWORD`, which `vecten secret` names but does not print; read it in the
console's Secrets Manager page.
[%- if 'platform' in components %]
To keep the login page off the open internet, list the networks that may reach it under
`[airflow] allow` in `vecten.toml`; everyone else gets 403 from NGINX, except on the health path
the ship polls. Run `vecten issues --env <env>` from a listed network.
[%- endif %]

### Admin access
[% if 'platform' in components and warehouse == 'postgres' %]
`vecten user add-admin <firstname> --env <env>` creates the warehouse login `admin_<firstname>`
with `rds_superuser` and CONNECT on `warehouse`, and stores its password as
`ADMIN_<FIRSTNAME>_PASSWORD` in the `admin` scope; it prints only the secret's id. It runs from
the laptop with your AWS login and needs the AWS CLI and its Session Manager plugin
(`session-manager-plugin`): it reaches the private instance through an SSM port-forwarding
tunnel via the setup host and signs in with the instance's master credential. Run again, it
keeps an existing login's password, or gives the login a new one when the secret is missing.
Read the password with
`aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id vecten/[[ project_name ]]/<env>/admin/ADMIN_<FIRSTNAME>_PASSWORD --query SecretString --output text`.
[% elif warehouse == 'snowflake' and 'platform' in components %]
`vecten user add-admin` covers Postgres warehouses only; create the admin user in Snowflake.
The tunnel below reaches the `state` instance.
[% else %]
The warehouse is your existing one, where the admin already has an account; vecten creates no
account there. There the MCP reads only the `core` and `marts` schemas:
`vecten mcp provisioner-script` grants on those two and nothing else, so the tables people
query belong there, as tables or views. The tunnel below reaches the `state` instance.
[% endif %]
For a manual `psql` session, open an SSM tunnel in one terminal; it needs the Session Manager
plugin and stays open until you stop it:

    aws ssm start-session --region <region> --target <setup host instance id> \
      --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost \
      --parameters host=<instance host>,portNumber=5432,localPortNumber=15432

and connect in another:
[% if 'platform' in components and warehouse == 'postgres' %]
    psql "host=127.0.0.1 port=15432 dbname=warehouse user=admin_<firstname> sslmode=require gssencmode=disable"
[% else %]
    psql "host=127.0.0.1 port=15432 dbname=postgres user=vecten_master sslmode=require gssencmode=disable"
[% endif %]
`.vecten/environments/<env>.json` names the setup host (`setup_host`), each host's instance id
(`hosts.<host>.instance_id`) and each instance's address (`postgres.<instance>.host`). The
master credential of an instance is the secret its `postgres.<instance>.master_secret` names,
which RDS creates and rotates; its JSON holds `username` (`vecten_master`) and `password`.
`sslmode=require` rather than `verify-full`, because the certificate names the RDS endpoint,
not `127.0.0.1`; the connection is still encrypted end to end.
`gssencmode=disable`, because a `psql` built with Kerberos support first asks for GSS
encryption, and through the tunnel that request hangs without an error.
[% if 'platform' in components and warehouse == 'postgres' %]
### Restore the warehouse

Every instance keeps automated backups with seven days of point-in-time recovery, and
`vecten destroy` adds a final snapshot. A restore creates a new RDS instance beside the
current one; Terraform then adopts it under the current one's name. Under `standard`, restoring
`warehouse` leaves the `state` instance, with Airflow's metadata[% if 'mcp' in components %] and
the MCP audit[% endif %], untouched.
Under `minimal` the one instance, `state`, holds all three databases, which return to the same
point together; read `state` for `warehouse` in the instance names and the Terraform address
below.

1. After a destroy, run `vecten deploy --env <env>` first, so the network, the subnet group and
   an empty instance exist.
2. Restore under a new identifier into the current instance's subnet group, security group and
   parameter group. `aws rds describe-db-instances --db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse`
   shows the security group id. A point-in-time restore needs the current instance, so after
   a destroy restore from the final snapshot. To a point in time:

       aws rds restore-db-instance-to-point-in-time \
         --source-db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse \
         --target-db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse-restored \
         --restore-time 2026-01-31T09:30:00Z \
         --db-subnet-group-name <prefix>-warehouse \
         --vpc-security-group-ids <security group id> \
         --db-parameter-group-name <prefix>-warehouse-pg[[ postgres_major ]] \
         --no-publicly-accessible

   Or from a snapshot; `aws rds describe-db-snapshots --db-instance-identifier
   <prefix>-warehouse` lists them, and a final snapshot is named
   `<prefix>-warehouse-final-<UTC yyyymmddHHMMSS>`:

       aws rds restore-db-instance-from-db-snapshot \
         --db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse-restored \
         --db-snapshot-identifier <snapshot> \
         --db-subnet-group-name <prefix>-warehouse \
         --vpc-security-group-ids <security group id> \
         --db-parameter-group-name <prefix>-warehouse-pg[[ postgres_major ]] \
         --no-publicly-accessible

   Then `aws rds wait db-instance-available --db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse-restored`.
3. Check the restored data through the tunnel above, with the restored instance's address from
   `aws rds describe-db-instances`, signing in as an `admin_<firstname>` login from
   `vecten user add-admin`: its password is in the restored data and in the secrets store.
4. Swap the names. Rename the current instance out of the way, wait for it, then give the
   restored one the current name; the address follows the name, so the stack reaches the
   restored data at the address it already uses:

       aws rds modify-db-instance --db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse \
         --new-db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse-old --apply-immediately
       aws rds wait db-instance-available --db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse-old
       aws rds modify-db-instance --db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse-restored \
         --new-db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse --apply-immediately

5. Point Terraform at the restored instance. A restored instance has no RDS-managed master
   secret, which the setup host reads and Terraform refuses to adopt an instance without, so
   turn it on first and wait until the secret's status reads `active`:

       aws rds modify-db-instance --db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse \
         --manage-master-user-password --apply-immediately
       aws rds describe-db-instances --db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse \
         --query 'DBInstances[0].MasterUserSecret.SecretStatus' --output text
       aws rds wait db-instance-available --db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse

   Terraform tracks an RDS instance by its resource id, which a rename keeps, so its state
   still names the old instance: remove that entry and import the restored instance by its
   name. From `deploy/terraform/aws`, with your AWS login and `state_bucket` from
   `[environments.<env>]` in `vecten.toml`:

       terraform init -reconfigure -backend-config=bucket=<state bucket> \
         -backend-config=key=<env>/aws.tfstate -backend-config=region=<region> \
         -backend-config=encrypt=true -backend-config=use_lockfile=true
       terraform state rm 'module.environment.module.postgres["warehouse"].aws_db_instance.this'
       terraform import -var-file=../../envs/<env>.tfvars \
         'module.environment.module.postgres["warehouse"].aws_db_instance.this' <prefix>-warehouse
[%- if 'mcp' in components and profile == 'standard' %]

   After a destroy, restore `state` as well, from the final snapshot the same destroy took, by
   steps 2 to 5 with `state` for `warehouse`: the deploy in step 1 created an empty `state`, so
   the MCP's registry of the people it signs in no longer matches their roles in the restored
   warehouse, and the MCP refuses to start until the two agree.
[%- endif %]

6. Run `vecten deploy --env <env>`. Terraform brings each restored instance to the module's
   settings, deletion protection and backups among them, the outputs follow, and the ship runs
   the setup SQL on the restored instances.
7. Once the restored instances serve, delete each old one. On `prod` lift its deletion
   protection first:

       aws rds modify-db-instance --db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse-old \
         --no-deletion-protection --apply-immediately
       aws rds delete-db-instance --db-instance-identifier <prefix>-warehouse-old \
         --final-snapshot-identifier <prefix>-warehouse-old-final
[% endif %]
### Destroy

`vecten destroy --env <env>` runs from the laptop with your AWS login. It asks you to type the
environment's name back; `--confirm <env>` gives the name without a prompt and is required with
`--json`, without a terminal and inside GitHub Actions. There is no `--yes`. Then it:

1. Takes a final snapshot of every RDS instance in the environment's outputs, named
   `<prefix>-<instance>-final-<UTC yyyymmddHHMMSS>`, waits until each is available and prints
   its identifier. `--delete-data <env>` takes no snapshot.
2. Lifts deletion protection with an apply of `destroying=true` limited to the RDS instances and
   the artifacts bucket that the Terraform state still holds, then runs `terraform destroy`.
3. Keeps the state bucket and every secret vecten stores, so a later `vecten deploy` finds its
   credentials (RDS deletes the master secrets it manages together with their instances),
   and clears `deployed_commit` in `vecten.toml`. Push that with `vecten github push`, so the
   `ship` workflow stops shipping.
[%- if 'mcp' in components %]
   A new deploy starts with an empty `state` database unless you restore its final snapshot,
   so destroy also clears in `vecten.toml` the MCP steps recorded there: the first user, the
   admin link and the connector grants. `vecten next` walks them again.
[%- endif %]

Running destroy again after a partial run is safe: a Terraform root whose state is empty is
reported and left alone, and an instance AWS reports gone gets neither a snapshot nor a
protection lift. Without stored outputs, destroy cannot tell which databases to snapshot and refuses unless `--delete-data <env>` is given. When the lift's
plan would create or delete anything, destroy refuses and applies nothing: the environment
differs from the committed `deploy/` files in a way that would replace a resource, and a person
reconciles the two, starting from `terraform plan` in `deploy/terraform/aws`, before running
destroy again.

### Choose before the first deploy

- `database_public_cidrs` in `deploy/envs/<env>.tfvars` opens the instances on port 5432 to the
  listed address ranges; the default, empty, keeps every instance private and reachable only
  through the tunnel. Decide before the first deploy: changing it later moves an instance's
  subnet group between the private and the public subnets, which RDS refuses for a running
  instance.
- `platform_size` sets the platform VM's tier, `small` by default, independently of `size`, and
  `instance_type_overrides` replaces the instance type of a host or an instance by its key.
- `root_volume_size_gb` sets the size of every VM's encrypted root volume, which holds the
  images and Docker's named volumes: 30 GiB by default, from 30 to 16384. A larger value grows
  each volume in place at the next deploy; EBS cannot shrink a volume, so a smaller value fails
  the apply. A running VM's file system does not see the added space until its partition and
  file system are grown; cloud-init grows both at boot, so a reboot should do it, which is not
  verified.
[%- if 'platform' in components %]
- `dags_paused_at_creation = true` makes the environment's new DAGs start paused, so nothing
  scheduled runs until someone unpauses it in the Airflow UI. By default a deployed DAG starts
  unpaused, because no deployed step unpauses it. The setting is read by `vecten ship`, not by
  Terraform, takes effect at the next ship, and leaves a DAG Airflow already knows as it is.
[%- endif %]
- The Terraform root's provider lock file, `deploy/terraform/aws/.terraform.lock.hcl`, is
  committed. The first local `vecten deploy --env <env> --plan-only` writes it; commit and push
  it with `vecten github push`, because `vecten deploy` refuses while `deploy/` holds changes
  GitHub does not have.
[% if 'platform' in components and warehouse == 'postgres' %]
### Postgres log prefix

The instance holding `warehouse` starts each log line with the session user and the application
name[% if 'mcp' in components %], so a person's query through the MCP shows `impersonator` as the
user and the person's warehouse name as the application[% endif %]. The module sets
`log_line_prefix` in that instance's parameter group to
`[% raw %]%m:%r:%u@%d:[%p]:%l:%e:%s:%v:%x:%c:%q%a:[% endraw %]`. RDS for PostgreSQL accepts only
two values for `log_line_prefix`, that one and its default, and refuses the same prefix without
the final colon. If a later RDS version refuses it, the apply fails on the parameter group; set
the RDS default in `deploy/envs/<env>.tfvars` and deploy again:

    [% raw %]log_line_prefix = "%t:%r:%u@%d:[%p]:"[% endraw %]

An instance that already runs picks up a changed prefix at its next reboot
(`aws rds reboot-db-instance --db-instance-identifier <prefix>-[% if profile == 'minimal' %]state[% else %]warehouse[% endif %]`).[% if 'mcp' in components %]
With the RDS default the person is still named by `application_name` in `pg_stat_activity` and
by the MCP audit.[% endif %]
[% endif %][% elif render_deploy and cloud == 'scaleway' %]
## Scaleway deployment

An environment such as `prod` runs in two Scaleway projects of your organization. The resources
project `<prefix>` holds an Instance, a virtual machine (VM), per host; Managed Database for
PostgreSQL instances on a private network; the environment's Secret Manager secrets; an Object
Storage bucket for bundles, dbt artifacts and Airflow logs; and a Domains and DNS record per
component (`airflow.<domain>` for the platform, `mcp.<domain>` for the MCP). The state project
`<prefix>-state` holds the Terraform state bucket and nothing else, so no key a VM holds can
reach the state. Under the `standard` profile the hosts are `platform` and `mcp`, one per
component the project has; under `minimal` one host, `main`, runs everything. The database
instances are `state`, which holds the `airflow` and `mcp_state` databases, and, when the
platform builds a Postgres warehouse, `warehouse`; under `minimal` the `state` instance holds
all three databases. Every resource sits in the region `deploy/envs/<env>.tfvars` names, and
every zonal one in the zone `<region>-1`. After a deploy, `.vecten/environments/<env>.json`
holds the environment's Terraform outputs: each host's server id and address, the setup host,
each instance's address and admin secret, and the bucket.

GitHub Actions does the deploying. `vecten deploy --env <env>` on the laptop dispatches the
`terraform-apply` workflow, which runs Terraform and then ships every host; every push to the
default branch runs the `ship` workflow, which ships the content without Terraform; a pull
request that changes `deploy/` or `vecten.toml` runs the `terraform-plan` workflow and gets the
plan as a comment. No VM accepts SSH and Scaleway offers no run-command API, so each VM pulls
its bundles from the bucket; "Shipping" below describes how.

The pull-request plan covers `prod`. The ship on every push covers the first environment of
the promotion order, `prod` when `vecten.toml` declares none, and each later environment of the
order ships only through `vecten promote --env <env>` ("Promotion order" below). Any other
environment is deployed and shipped when you dispatch it with `vecten deploy --env <env>` or
`vecten ship --env <env>`. Each ship recreates the stack's
containers on the host, so Airflow tasks running at that moment restart. A ship that fails
on the host prints, after its error, the last lines of `docker compose`'s own output (a failed
pull, build or port) and of each failing container's log, with secrets masked; on a VM without
a shell, that is where a failed start shows its cause.

In the commands below, `<prefix>` is `vecten-<project>-<env>`, the project's and the
environment's names lowercased with each run of other characters as one `-`, the project part
shortened when the two names are long; `<region>` is the region in `deploy/envs/<env>.tfvars`;
`<project id>` and `<state project id>` are `cloud_ids["project"]` and
`cloud_ids["state_project"]` under `[environments.<env>]` in `vecten.toml`; and `scw` is the
Scaleway CLI, logged in with `scw init`.

### What `vecten bootstrap` creates

`vecten bootstrap --env <env>` runs once per environment, from the laptop, with your own
Scaleway login: `scw init`, or `SCW_ACCESS_KEY`, `SCW_SECRET_KEY` and
`SCW_DEFAULT_ORGANIZATION_ID` in the environment. It creates projects and IAM applications,
policies and keys, so it needs an administrator's key, such as the Organization Owner's or one
holding IAMManager and the right to create projects. Running it again is safe. It creates or
updates:

- The two projects, each claimed by its description, `vecten:<role>:<project>:<env>` with
  `<role>` `resources` or `state`. A project of the expected name whose description differs is
  refused, and the message names the description bootstrap expects; set it in the project's
  settings when the project is this environment's. An environment name ending in `-state` is
  refused, because `<prefix>-state` names a state project.
- The Terraform state bucket `vecten-<project>-<env>-state-<8 hex>`, the last part the start of
  the state project's id: versioned, with versions older than the current one expiring after
  90 days. `vecten destroy` keeps it. Its bucket policy lets the deploy application read and
  write every object, the plan application read every object and write only the
  `<env>/*.tflock` lock objects, and the operator who ran bootstrap manage the bucket. The
  policy names its operator, so another operator's rerun replaces the first one in it, and the
  first gets the bucket back by running bootstrap again; the Organization Owner can always
  rewrite the policy. `vecten deploy --plan-only` and `vecten destroy` on the laptop read the
  state with your key, so they need you to be the operator the policy names.
- IAM applications and their policies. None holds an IAM permission set, so no key CI holds can
  widen a key, its own included.
  - `<prefix>-deploy`, which the apply and ship jobs sign with: full access to Instances,
    Block Storage (the volumes POP2 servers boot from), private networks, Managed Databases,
    Secret Manager and Object Storage in the resources project, read access to its IP
    addresses, full access to Domains and DNS in the project holding the DNS zone, and object
    access in the state project.
  - `<prefix>-plan`, which pull-request plans sign with: object access in the state project
    only, which the bucket policy cuts down to reading the state and writing lock objects. It
    reads nothing in the resources project, so a pull-request plan runs without a refresh and
    compares the change with the stored state, not with the live resources.
  - `<prefix>-<host>` per host, described in "The VM's key".
- An API key per application, expiring 365 days after its creation; "Keys and rotation" covers
  renewing them.
- On GitHub: the environment `<env>` with the variables `VECTEN_SCW_PROJECT_ID`,
  `VECTEN_SCW_STATE_PROJECT_ID`, `VECTEN_SCW_REGION` and `VECTEN_STATE_BUCKET`, and the deploy
  key as its secrets `VECTEN_SCW_ACCESS_KEY` and `VECTEN_SCW_SECRET_KEY`; and the plan key as
  the repository secrets `VECTEN_SCW_PLAN_ACCESS_KEY_<ENV>` and
  `VECTEN_SCW_PLAN_SECRET_KEY_<ENV>`, the environment's name uppercased with `-` as `_`.
  Scaleway IAM offers no OIDC federation, so these keys are how GitHub Actions signs in. A
  repository secret reaches every workflow run a pushed branch starts, which is why the plan key
  holds only what a plan needs.
- In `vecten.toml`, under `[environments.<env>]`: `bootstrapped`, `state_bucket` and
  `cloud_ids`, the ids of the projects, the applications and the CI keys and when each key
  expires (`expires_deploy`, `expires_plan`, `expires_<host>`), and the operator who ran
  bootstrap (`operator_user` or `operator_application`), none of them secret. `vecten deploy`
  gives that operator full access to the artifacts bucket, whose policy otherwise names no
  person: a laptop plan refreshes the bucket, `vecten destroy` empties and deletes it, and
  `vecten issues --env <env>` reads dbt's run results there. After another person runs
  bootstrap, the next deploy hands that access to them.

The DNS zone `dns_zone` in `deploy/envs/<env>.tfvars` names must exist in Domains and DNS in
one of the organization's projects, such as its default project, before bootstrap runs.
Bootstrap finds that project through the Domains and DNS API, gives the deploy key full access
to Domains and DNS there, and records it as `cloud_ids["dns_project"]`, which `vecten deploy`
hands to Terraform. When no project of the organization holds the zone, bootstrap creates
nothing and exits naming the zone.

Run `vecten bootstrap --env <env>` again after changing `components` or `profile`, then
`vecten deploy --env <env>` straight after. Bootstrap gives a new host its application and key
and deletes those of a host the configuration no longer has; that host's VM keeps running until
the deploy replaces it, and without its key its containers lose access to their secrets and the
bucket.

### Protect the deploy environment

The GitHub environment `<env>` is the deploy key's only gate: GitHub hands the environment's
secrets to any workflow job in this repository that declares `environment: <env>`, and the
deploy key can change everything in the resources project. When `vecten bootstrap` creates the
environment, it limits deployments to the default branch.
GitHub offers that rule, a deployment branch policy, on public repositories and on paid plans.
On a private repository whose plan lacks it, bootstrap leaves the environment unrestricted and
prints a warning naming this section; anyone who can push a branch can then run a job that
deploys. Protect the environment by hand under the repository's Settings, Environments, `<env>`:
add required reviewers where the plan offers them, or move to a plan that offers branch
policies and limit the environment to the default branch. Bootstrap never rewrites an existing
environment, so rules added by hand survive a rerun; when an environment it finds has no
deployment branch restriction, it prints the same warning and leaves it as it is.

### Keys and rotation

Every key bootstrap creates, the deploy key, the plan key and each VM's key, expires 365 days
after its creation, and bootstrap records each expiry in `vecten.toml` as
`cloud_ids["expires_<owner>"]`. Once a key is within 30 days of its expiry, `vecten next` leads
with `vecten bootstrap --env <env>` and `vecten deploy` prints a warning naming the key and the
date; the deploy itself goes on. An expired key stops whatever signs with it: CI's jobs for the
deploy and plan keys, a VM's containers and watcher for its key.

Run `vecten bootstrap --env <env>` within those 30 days to rotate the key; a key further from
its expiry is kept. A rotated CI key's successor goes to GitHub, and the old key stays valid,
recorded as `cloud_ids["retiring_deploy"]` or `cloud_ids["retiring_plan"]`, so a workflow run
already under way finishes with it; the next bootstrap run deletes it. A rotated VM key's
successor becomes the newest version of the host's key secret; the next `vecten deploy` hands it
to the VM, whose watcher acknowledges it and recreates the stack's containers with it, and
`vecten next` leads with that deploy until a deploy from the laptop has run. The old
key stays valid until then, because the running containers sign with it, and the next bootstrap
after the acknowledgement deletes it. Bootstrap's result lists the hosts that have not
acknowledged a new key under `awaiting_ack`. `scw iam api-key list` shows each key's expiry.

An organization can enforce a maximum API key duration (Organization settings, Security,
Credentials maximum duration); one shorter than 365 days makes Scaleway refuse every key
bootstrap creates, and bootstrap then exits naming that setting. Keep the maximum at 365 days
or more.

Every Scaleway organization has a group, "All Applications", that holds every IAM application.
A policy attached to it applies to the deploy, plan and VM keys as well and widens what each can
reach, so attach none to it in an organization that runs vecten.

### The VM's key

Scaleway has no instance identity: a VM signs its calls with an API key of its own application,
`<prefix>-<host>`. This key, kept on the VM's disk, is the one exception to the rule that no
long-lived credential sits on a disk. Its policy reaches the resources project only: it reads
the VM's own secret scopes and may list secret names and metadata there, never another scope's
value, because Secret Manager finds a secret by listing before it reads it; it adds versions to
the secrets the VM generates; and it reads and writes the artifacts bucket, which the bucket's
policy cuts down to the VM's bundles and its components' prefixes.

`vecten bootstrap` creates the key and stores it in Secret Manager as `HOST_KEY_<HOST>` in the
`deploy` scope, which only the deploy key reads. After Terraform applies, `vecten deploy` reads
it and sets it on the server as the user data entry `vecten-key` through the Instances API,
never through Terraform. The VM's watcher reads the entry from the metadata server, writes it to
`/var/lib/vecten/scw-key` (owned by root, mode 0600), where it survives a reboot, and
acknowledges it by writing `<first component>/key-ack/<SHA-256 of the access key>.json` to the
bucket. `vecten deploy` waits up to 15 minutes for that object, then deletes the user data
entry, because the Terraform provider copies every user data entry into state on a refresh; a
missing acknowledgement fails the deploy naming the host, and the entry is deleted all the same.
Every deploy and every destroy also deletes a `vecten-key` entry a cancelled deploy left
behind, before Terraform refreshes. Containers receive the key in their environment, as
`SCW_ACCESS_KEY` and `SCW_SECRET_KEY` for Secret Manager and as the S3 credentials for the
bucket and Airflow's remote logs.

A rule in Docker's `DOCKER-USER` chain drops container traffic to the metadata server, so a
container cannot read the user data entry. Two gaps remain. The setup SQL runs in a container
on the host's network (`docker run --network host`), which that rule does not filter. After a
reboot, dockerd restarts the containers before the systemd unit adds the rule. Both matter only
while the key sits in the metadata, which is the placement window between `vecten deploy`
setting the entry and deleting it after the acknowledgement, so the risk is low.

### Secrets

Every secret has an environment-variable-style name, and its prefix picks the scope it is
stored under in the resources project's Secret Manager: the secret `<NAME>` at the path
`/vecten/[[ project_name ]]/<env>/<scope>`, which IAM sees as
`/vecten/[[ project_name ]]/<env>/<scope>/<NAME>`:

| Name | Scope | Read by a VM |
|---|---|---|
| `MCP_*` | `mcp` | the MCP VM |
| `DEPLOY_*` | `deploy` | none: credentials CI deploys with; bootstrap also keeps the VM keys here |
| `ADMIN_*` | `admin` | none: passwords from `vecten user add-admin` |
| `SETUP_*` | `setup` | the setup host: each database instance's admin credential |
| any other name | `platform` | the platform VM |

Under `minimal` the one VM reads `platform`, `mcp` and `setup`. The deploy key reads and writes
every scope, as does an administrator's own login. `vecten secret set <NAME> --env <env>`
stores a secret from a hidden prompt or standard input, creating it at its scope's path when it
does not exist yet, `--from-local` copies it from `.env`, and `vecten secret list --env <env>`
prints names only.

The setup host is the one VM that reads the database instances' admin credentials: the MCP VM
when the project has the `mcp` component, else the platform VM, and `main` under `minimal`.
Each instance's credential is the secret `SETUP_<INSTANCE>_ADMIN_PASSWORD`, a JSON with
`username` (`vecten_admin`) and `password`. Terraform generates each password in its own memory
during an apply and hands it to the instance and to the secret through write-only arguments, so
neither the state nor a plan holds it; raising `state_admin_password_version` or
`warehouse_admin_password_version` in `deploy/envs/<env>.tfvars` sets a new password on that
instance and in its secret together. The admin password in Secret Manager is the truth: every
`vecten deploy` resets the instance to it after the apply, so a password changed in the console
is overwritten on the next deploy. On every ship the setup host runs the setup SQL on each
instance, which creates the databases, their owner roles and the service roles and is safe to
repeat, and it generates the database passwords the stack uses: `AIRFLOW_DB_PASSWORD` and
`LOADER_PASSWORD` in the `platform` scope, `MCP_STATE_DB_PASSWORD`, `MCP_PROVISIONER_PASSWORD`
and `MCP_IMPERSONATOR_PASSWORD` in the `mcp` scope, each only when the project has what it
serves. Terraform creates each of those secrets empty, so a VM key only ever adds a version to a
secret that exists; the setup host's key may add versions to and read back exactly those names,
so that a replaced instance gets the same passwords again. The platform VM generates the Airflow
application secrets (`AIRFLOW_FERNET_KEY`, `AIRFLOW_API_SECRET_KEY`, `AIRFLOW_JWT_SECRET`,
`AIRFLOW_ADMIN_PASSWORD`, `AIRFLOW_BASIC_AUTH_PASSWORD`) on its first ship, and its key may add
versions to exactly those five names, which DAG code on that VM can do as well. When an MCP VM
exists, the platform VM, which runs DAG code, holds no admin credential.

Containers receive their secrets as environment variables. Docker keeps each container's
environment in `/var/lib/docker/containers/<id>/config.v2.json`, readable by root only. The two
files the stack needs with secret content, the Airflow passwords file and a basic-auth file
that only an older release's NGINX reads, kept so a rollback to one finds it, live under
`/run/vecten/`, a memory-backed directory (tmpfs) with mode 0600 that the VM agent rebuilds on
every ship and at boot. Apart from those and the VM's key, vecten writes no file holding a
secret on a VM.

Airflow's own login guards `https://airflow.[[ domain ]]`: user `admin`, password
`AIRFLOW_ADMIN_PASSWORD`, which `vecten secret` names but does not print; read it in the
console's Secret Manager page of the project `<prefix>`.
[%- if 'platform' in components %]
To keep the login page off the open internet, list the networks that may reach it under
`[airflow] allow` in `vecten.toml`; everyone else gets 403 from NGINX, except on the health path
the ship polls. Run `vecten issues --env <env>` from a listed network.
[%- endif %]

### Shipping

CI never reaches into a VM. `vecten ship` uploads each host's bundle to
`bundles/<host>/<commit>.tar.gz` in the artifacts bucket and rewrites
`bundles/<host>/pointer.json` with the commit and the time of the request. On each VM the
systemd timer `vecten-watch.timer` runs the watcher every 15 seconds; when the pointer names a
request it has not handled, the watcher downloads the bundle, ships it with the same VM agent
AWS VMs run, and writes `<first component>/ship-status/<commit>.json` with the outcome and the
agent's last line. CI waits for that status, 15 minutes for a host's first ship and 10 minutes
after, and 15 minutes for every host the same `vecten deploy` handed a key to, since its
server may be new; then it polls each component's health URL. When a poll fails, CI points the
host back at the previous commit, waits for its status, and fails naming the component. A
failed agent run restores the previous release by itself and says so in its line; its status
names the commit the host runs afterwards, and CI points the pointer at that commit, so the
pointer always names the host's last good release and a later rollback never lands on a
rejected one. A rejected first ship leaves no pointer. A ship of the commit a host already runs
marks the pointer `force`, which makes the watcher ship it again and recreate its containers. A
ship that finds no status within its window fails naming the host: the VM may still be booting,
or may not hold its key yet, which `vecten deploy` hands it.

A server that replaced another starts with no release and finds the pointer its predecessor
shipped. Its watcher skips a pointer requested before the moment it acknowledged its key, which
it takes from the modification time of its acknowledgement marker in `/var/lib/vecten`, and
ships the newer pointer the same deploy writes. This compares CI's clock with the VM's, both
kept by NTP.

The VM's user data writes the watcher's settings, `/etc/vecten/watch.json` (the host, its
components, the bucket, the region, the project and the secret prefix), at first boot only, and
the watcher files its acknowledgements and statuses under the first component listed there.
Under `minimal`, a change to `components` that changes the first one, such as adding `platform`
to an MCP-only project, leaves ships and key handovers waiting on that VM until it is replaced.

### Network

One VPC private network holds the VMs and the database instances. Each VM has a flexible IP and
a security group that drops every inbound connection except on ports 80 and 443; outbound
traffic is open. Security groups filter public traffic only: inside the private network every
VM reaches every instance on any port, and the database credentials are the gate. The
instances have no public endpoint unless `database_public_cidrs` lists address ranges.

### Admin access

Scaleway offers no SSH and no run-command API to tunnel through, and a private network admits
no laptop, so admin access goes through the instances' public endpoint. It exists only while
`database_public_cidrs` in `deploy/envs/<env>.tfvars` lists the address ranges you connect
from, for example `["203.0.113.7/32"]`: `vecten deploy` adds the endpoint with those ranges as
its allow-list, and once the setting is empty again the next deploy deletes it.
[% if 'platform' in components and warehouse == 'postgres' %]
`vecten user add-admin <firstname> --env <env>` creates the warehouse login `admin_<firstname>`
as a member of `warehouse_owner` and `loader` with CONNECT on `warehouse`, and stores its
password as `ADMIN_<FIRSTNAME>_PASSWORD` in the `admin` scope; it prints only the secret's id.
Managed Databases grant no superuser, and those two roles own and load every warehouse object.
It runs from the laptop with your Scaleway login and refuses while `database_public_cidrs` is
empty. It signs in as `vecten_admin` with the credential in
`SETUP_[% if profile == 'minimal' %]STATE[% else %]WAREHOUSE[% endif %]_ADMIN_PASSWORD`, checks the
server against the instance's own certificate authority, which it fetches from the Managed
Databases API, with `sslmode=verify-ca` (the certificate names the instance's private address,
not the public endpoint, so `verify-full` fails and the name goes unchecked), and accepts SCRAM
authentication only. Run
again, it keeps an existing login's password, or gives the login a new one when the secret is
missing.
[% elif warehouse == 'snowflake' and 'platform' in components %]
`vecten user add-admin` covers Postgres warehouses only; create the admin user in Snowflake.
The endpoint below reaches the `state` instance.
[% else %]
The warehouse is your existing one, where the admin already has an account; vecten creates no
account there. There the MCP reads only the `core` and `marts` schemas:
`vecten mcp provisioner-script` grants on those two and nothing else, so the tables people
query belong there, as tables or views. The endpoint below reaches the `state` instance.
[% endif %]
For a manual `psql` session, find the instance, read its public endpoint's address and port in
the `endpoints` that `scw rdb instance get` prints, save its certificate authority, and connect:

    scw rdb instance list region=<region> project-id=<project id> name=<prefix>-<instance>
    scw rdb instance get <instance id> region=<region>
    scw rdb instance get-certificate <instance id> region=<region> > ca.pem
    psql "host=<endpoint address> port=<endpoint port> dbname=[% if 'platform' in components and warehouse == 'postgres' %]warehouse user=admin_<firstname>[% else %]postgres user=vecten_admin[% endif %] sslmode=verify-ca sslrootcert=ca.pem"

The password is [% if 'platform' in components and warehouse == 'postgres' %]the value of `ADMIN_<FIRSTNAME>_PASSWORD`[% else %]the `password` field of `SETUP_STATE_ADMIN_PASSWORD`[% endif %], shown in the console's
Secret Manager page of the project `<prefix>`.
[% if 'platform' in components and warehouse == 'postgres' %]
### Restore the warehouse

Every instance keeps a daily backup of each database for seven days, and `vecten destroy` adds a
final snapshot of every instance. Managed Databases offer no point-in-time recovery, so a
restore returns to a backup or a snapshot. A restore from a snapshot creates a new instance
beside the current one; Terraform then adopts it under the current one's name. Under
`standard`, restoring `warehouse` leaves the `state` instance, with Airflow's metadata[% if 'mcp' in components %] and the
MCP audit[% endif %], untouched.
Under `minimal` the one instance, `state`, holds all three databases, which return to the same
point together; read `state` for `warehouse` in the instance names and the Terraform address
below.

1. After a destroy, run `vecten deploy --env <env>` first, so the network, the secrets and an
   empty instance exist.
2. Create an instance from the snapshot. `scw rdb snapshot list region=<region>
   project-id=<project id>` lists the snapshots, which outlive their instance until their
   `expires_at`: Scaleway deletes a snapshot at most 365 days after it was taken and accepts no
   later date. To keep one longer, restore it by this step and take a new snapshot of the
   restored instance (`scw rdb snapshot create instance-id=<restored instance id>
   region=<region> name=<name>`). A final snapshot is named
   `<prefix>-warehouse-final-<UTC yyyymmddHHMMSS>`. Take the node type from
   `scw rdb instance get` of the current instance:

       scw rdb snapshot restore <snapshot id> region=<region> \
         instance-name=<prefix>-warehouse-restored node-type=<node type> is-ha-cluster=false
       scw rdb instance wait <restored instance id> region=<region>

   The restored instance comes back with a public endpoint and no private one. Limit the
   public endpoint's allowed addresses to your own until step 5:

       scw rdb acl set <your address>/32 instance-id=<restored instance id> region=<region>
3. Rename the current instance out of the way, so the name `<prefix>-warehouse` names one
   instance only:

       scw rdb instance update <current instance id> region=<region> name=<prefix>-warehouse-old

4. Point Terraform at the restored instance. Terraform tracks a Managed Database instance by its
   id, so remove the current one from the state and import the restored one. From
   `deploy/terraform/scaleway`, as the operator the state bucket's policy names, with your
   Scaleway login for the provider and your key for the state backend, whose credentials take
   the form `<access key>@<state project id>`; `<app ids>` are the `cloud_ids["app_<key>"]`
   values in `vecten.toml`, as `{deploy="<id>",plan="<id>",<host>="<id>"}` for every key, and
   `<dns project id>` is `cloud_ids["dns_project"]`. The module reads the instance's
   private-network endpoint, so the import fails on `private_network` until the restored
   instance has one; attach it to the environment's network first, whose id
   `scw vpc private-network list region=<region> project-id=<project id> name=<prefix>` prints:

       scw rdb endpoint create <restored instance id> region=<region> \
         private-network.private-network-id=<private network id> private-network.enable-ipam=true
       scw rdb instance wait <restored instance id> region=<region>
       export AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<access key>@<state project id> AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<secret key>
       terraform init -reconfigure -backend-config=bucket=<state bucket> \
         -backend-config=key=<env>/scaleway.tfstate
       terraform state rm 'module.environment.module.postgres["warehouse"].scaleway_rdb_instance.this'
       terraform import -var-file=../../envs/<env>.tfvars -var project_id=<project id> \
         -var dns_project_id=<dns project id> -var 'application_ids=<app ids>' \
         'module.environment.module.postgres["warehouse"].scaleway_rdb_instance.this' \
         <region>/<restored instance id>

   When `database_public_cidrs` is set, also remove the current instance's allow-list:
   `terraform state rm 'module.environment.module.postgres["warehouse"].scaleway_rdb_acl.public[0]'`.

   The restored instance keeps its snapshot's admin password, and the next deploy resets it to
   the password in `SETUP_[% if profile == 'minimal' %]STATE[% else %]WAREHOUSE[% endif %]_ADMIN_PASSWORD`.
[%- if 'mcp' in components and profile == 'standard' %]

   After a destroy, restore `state` as well, from the final snapshot the same destroy took, by
   steps 2 to 4 with `state` for `warehouse`: the deploy in step 1 created an empty `state`, so
   the MCP's registry of the people it signs in no longer matches their roles in the restored
   warehouse, and the MCP refuses to start until the two agree.
[%- endif %]
5. Run `vecten deploy --env <env> --plan-only` and read `.vecten/plans/<env>.txt`: the
   instance must show as updated in place (`~`), never as replaced (`-/+`), because a
   replacement would create an empty instance in its stead. An import leaves the instance's
   `password_wo_version` empty in state, so the plan also sets it, and the admin secret's
   version is replaced alongside. Then run
   `vecten deploy --env <env>`. Terraform gives the restored instance the module's name,
   network and settings, the outputs follow, and the ship runs the setup SQL on it. When
   `database_public_cidrs` is empty, the deploy deletes any public endpoint the instance has.
   After a destroy this deploy's ship fails and rolls back, because the services sign in with
   passwords the restored roles do not have (`password authentication failed`); step 6 fixes
   that.
6. After a destroy, also reset the service roles' passwords: destroy deleted the secrets
   Terraform created, so the deploy in step 1 generated new passwords while the restored roles
   keep the old ones. Read each new value in the console's Secret Manager page of the project
   `<prefix>`; `vecten secret` prints names only. With `database_public_cidrs` set, sign in as
   `vecten_admin` as "Admin access" shows and run:

       ALTER ROLE loader PASSWORD '<value of LOADER_PASSWORD>';
[%- if 'mcp' in components %]
       ALTER ROLE provisioner PASSWORD '<value of MCP_PROVISIONER_PASSWORD>';
       ALTER ROLE impersonator PASSWORD '<value of MCP_IMPERSONATOR_PASSWORD>';
[%- endif %]
[%- if profile == 'minimal' %]
       ALTER ROLE airflow PASSWORD '<value of AIRFLOW_DB_PASSWORD>';
[%- if 'mcp' in components %]
       ALTER ROLE mcp_state PASSWORD '<value of MCP_STATE_DB_PASSWORD>';
[%- endif %]
[%- elif 'mcp' in components %]

   And on the restored `state` instance:

       ALTER ROLE airflow PASSWORD '<value of AIRFLOW_DB_PASSWORD>';
       ALTER ROLE mcp_state PASSWORD '<value of MCP_STATE_DB_PASSWORD>';
[%- endif %]

   Then run `vecten deploy --env <env>` again: it ships with the passwords the roles now have
   and finishes the steps the failed run left.
[%- if profile == 'minimal' or 'mcp' in components %] The restored `state` instance holds
   Airflow's metadata, and the deploy generated a new `AIRFLOW_FERNET_KEY`, so the Airflow
   connections and variables stored encrypted with the old key can no longer be read: set them
   again in Airflow.
[%- endif %]
7. Check the restored data, signing in as an `admin_<firstname>` login from
   `vecten user add-admin`: its password is in the restored data and in the secrets store. Once
   the restored instances serve, delete each old one:

       scw rdb instance delete <old instance id> region=<region>

A daily backup restores one database into an instance of your choosing, under another
database name, for example to copy rows back by hand:
`scw rdb backup list region=<region> instance-id=<instance id>` lists them, and
`scw rdb backup restore <backup id> region=<region> instance-id=<instance id> database-name=warehouse_restored`
restores one.
[% endif %]
### Destroy

`vecten destroy --env <env>` runs from the laptop with your Scaleway login, as the operator the
state bucket's policy names. It asks you to type the environment's name back; `--confirm <env>`
gives the name without a prompt and is required with `--json`, without a terminal and inside
GitHub Actions. There is no `--yes`. Then it:

1. Takes a final snapshot of every database instance in the environment's outputs through the
   Managed Databases API, named `<prefix>-<instance>-final-<UTC yyyymmddHHMMSS>`, waits until
   each is ready and prints its name, then the date Scaleway deletes them: 365 days after they
   were taken, the longest it keeps a snapshot. To keep the data longer, restore a snapshot
   before then[% if 'platform' in components and warehouse == 'postgres' %] ("Restore the warehouse", step 2)[% endif %] and take a new snapshot of the restored
   instance. `--delete-data <env>` takes no
   snapshot. Managed Databases offer no deletion protection, so these snapshots are the
   databases' only guard.
2. Applies `destroying=true` to the artifacts bucket that the Terraform state still holds, so
   Terraform may empty it, then runs `terraform destroy`.
3. Keeps both projects, the state bucket, the applications and keys bootstrap made, the VM keys
   in the `deploy` scope, and the secrets `vecten secret set` and `vecten user add-admin`
   stored. The secrets Terraform created go with the environment: the instances' admin
   credentials and the passwords the VMs generate. It clears `deployed_commit` in
   `vecten.toml`; push that with `vecten github push`, so the `ship` workflow stops shipping.
[%- if 'mcp' in components %]
   A new deploy starts with an empty `state` database unless you restore its final snapshot,
   so destroy also clears in `vecten.toml` the MCP steps recorded there: the first user, the
   admin link and the connector grants. `vecten next` walks them again.
[%- endif %]

Running destroy again after a partial run is safe: a Terraform root whose state is empty is
reported and left alone, and an instance Scaleway reports gone gets no snapshot. Without stored
outputs, destroy cannot tell which databases to snapshot and refuses unless
`--delete-data <env>` is given. When the plan of step 2 would create or delete anything,
destroy refuses and applies nothing: the environment differs from the committed `deploy/` files
in a way that would replace a resource, and a person reconciles the two, starting from
`terraform plan` in `deploy/terraform/scaleway`, before running destroy again.

### Choose before the first deploy

- `region` in `deploy/envs/<env>.tfvars` must equal `region` under `[deploy]` in
  `vecten.toml`, which was written into `deploy/terraform/scaleway/backend.tf` when the deploy
  layer was rendered and in which bootstrap creates the state bucket; bootstrap, deploy and
  destroy refuse a tfvars `region` that differs, naming both values.
- `database_public_cidrs` opens every instance's public endpoint to the listed address ranges;
  the default, empty, keeps every instance on the private network only. `vecten user add-admin`
  needs it. Adding the endpoint to a running instance is not verified to happen in place, so
  set it before the first deploy when you will want admin access.
- `platform_size` sets the platform VM's tier, `small` by default, independently of `size`, and
  `instance_type_overrides` replaces the server or node type of a host or an instance by its
  key: `platform`, `mcp`, `main`, `state` or `warehouse`.
- `root_volume_size_gb` sets the size of every VM's boot volume, which holds the images and
  Docker's named volumes: 30 GB by default, and at least 30. A DEV1 type boots from its local
  SSD, so the size cannot exceed the type's local storage (40 GB on DEV1-M, 80 GB on DEV1-L);
  a POP2 type boots from an SBS volume, which can grow but never shrink. Whether a changed size
  is applied in place or replaces the VM is not verified, so set it before the first deploy.
[%- if 'platform' in components %]
- `dags_paused_at_creation = true` makes the environment's new DAGs start paused, so nothing
  scheduled runs until someone unpauses it in the Airflow UI. By default a deployed DAG starts
  unpaused, because no deployed step unpauses it. The setting is read by `vecten ship`, not by
  Terraform, takes effect at the next ship, and leaves a DAG Airflow already knows as it is.
[%- endif %]
- The Terraform root's provider lock file, `deploy/terraform/scaleway/.terraform.lock.hcl`, is
  committed. The first local `vecten deploy --env <env> --plan-only` writes it; commit and push
  it with `vecten github push`, because `vecten deploy` refuses while `deploy/` holds changes
  GitHub does not have.

### Changes that replace a server or a database

- A change of a VM's server type, through `size`, `platform_size` or
  `instance_type_overrides`, replaces the VM rather than migrating it: Scaleway cannot move a
  server between the DEV1 types (the `small` tier, and the `minimal` profile's VM), which boot
  from a local volume, and the POP2 types (`medium` and `large`), which boot from a Block
  Storage (SBS) volume. The VM keeps its flexible IP, so its hostnames keep resolving; the new
  server gets its key and its release from the same `vecten deploy`, and anything kept only on
  the old server's disk is lost.
- An SBS volume can grow but never shrink. Lowering a POP2 VM's size or a database instance's
  storage takes a new volume, so it replaces the VM, or the instance by the procedure below.
- An apply that would delete or replace a database instance is refused: `vecten deploy` exits
  4 naming the instance and applies nothing, because Managed Databases offer no deletion
  protection. Revert the change that causes it, or replace the instance deliberately, by hand:
  take a snapshot (`scw rdb snapshot create instance-id=<instance id> region=<region>
  name=<prefix>-<instance>-before-replace`), wait until `scw rdb snapshot get <snapshot id>
  region=<region>` reports `ready`, run `terraform apply` in `deploy/terraform/scaleway` with
  the deploy key, then restore the data from the snapshot as "Restore the warehouse" describes.
[% if 'platform' in components and warehouse == 'postgres' %]
### Postgres log prefix

Managed Databases do not let a customer set `log_line_prefix`, so the instance holding
`warehouse` logs in Scaleway's own format, which the module leaves as it is:
`[% raw %]%t [%p]: user=%u,db=%d,app=%a,client=%h[% endraw %]`. At the instance's default settings the log
holds checkpoints and server events but no line per client session or failed statement, so it
does not trace a query to a person.[% if 'mcp' in components %]
A person's query through the MCP runs as `impersonator` with the person's warehouse name as
`application_name`: it names the person in `pg_stat_activity`, `pg_stat_statements` attributes
the query to the person's role, and the MCP audit records it; the audit is the record of who
ran what.[% endif %] Cockpit's PostgreSQL dashboard can show no data for the project; read the
instance's log from the Managed Databases API instead, a whole UTC day per file:

    scw rdb log download <instance id> region=<region> from=<RFC 3339> to=<RFC 3339> output=<file>
[% endif %][% endif %][% if render_deploy %]
### One queue per environment

Apply and ship runs for an environment share the GitHub Actions concurrency group
`deploy-<env>`. GitHub runs one at a time and keeps at most one pending run per group, so a
newer run cancels the pending one: a ship queued behind a running apply can cancel a pending
apply, or the reverse. `vecten deploy` on the laptop then reports that its run ended
`cancelled`, with the run's address; run it again.

### Certificates

On a host's first ship the VM agent waits up to five minutes for each of its hostnames to
resolve to the VM's address, then requests a Let's Encrypt certificate with certbot, at most
three attempts per ship. Let's Encrypt allows five failed validations per hostname per hour, so
two failed ships within an hour can reach that limit; wait an hour before shipping again. The
module creates A records only, so a stale AAAA (IPv6) record for the same name in the zone can
send the validation to another server: remove it. A systemd timer on the VM renews the
certificates daily.
[% if warehouse == 'snowflake' and 'platform' in components %]
### Snowflake

`vecten deploy` also applies the Terraform root `deploy/terraform/snowflake`, with its state at
`<env>/snowflake.tfstate` in the state bucket. It creates in your Snowflake account the
database, the schemas `STAGING`, `SNAPSHOTS`, `CORE` and `MARTS`, the roles `ANALYST`, `LOADER`
and, with the `mcp` component, `PROVISIONER`, the service user `LOADER`, and a warehouse named
`<PROJECT>_<ENV>_WH`.

- The account is the `ORGANIZATION-ACCOUNT` identifier in `snowflake_account` in
  `deploy/envs/<env>.tfvars` and `account` under `[warehouse]` in `vecten.toml`, which
  `vecten deploy` refuses unless they agree. `vecten answer snowflake_account <ORG-ACCOUNT>`
  followed by `vecten init --deploy` writes both for `prod`.
- One environment per Snowflake account: the role names, the `LOADER` user and the database's
  name are the same for every environment, so a second environment in the same account collides
  with the first. They also collide with roles or a user of the same names already in the
  account; check before the first deploy.
- Terraform signs in as a deploy user with key-pair authentication. Create that user with a
  default role that holds
  CREATE DATABASE, CREATE WAREHOUSE, CREATE ROLE, CREATE USER and MANAGE GRANTS
  on the account. MANAGE GRANTS lets the first apply set the future grants in `CORE` and
  `MARTS` through which `ANALYST` reads every table dbt builds there. Then store the user's
  name and private key:
  `vecten secret set DEPLOY_SNOWFLAKE_USER --env <env>` and
  `vecten secret set DEPLOY_SNOWFLAKE_PRIVATE_KEY --env <env> < <private key file>`.
  `vecten deploy` refuses while either is missing.
- The first plan generates the `LOADER` user's key pair and stores the private key as
  `SNOWFLAKE_LOADER_PRIVATE_KEY`; only the public key reaches Terraform.
- Pull-request plans cover the [% if cloud == 'aws' %]AWS root only, because the plan role reads no `deploy` secret[% else %]Scaleway root only, because the plan key reads no secret[% endif %];
  the Snowflake root is planned when the `terraform-apply` workflow is dispatched.
- `vecten destroy` keeps the Snowflake database, and its Terraform root, unless
  `--delete-data <env>` is given.
[%- if 'mcp' in components %] Either way the users and roles `PROVISIONER` created stay,
  and destroy clears the provisioner script's confirmation, so the next deploy ships no host
  until the script has run again. Its last query lists a `DROP` for each user and role
  `PROVISIONER` owns: after a restore of the `state` snapshot they are the MCP's own and stay;
  without one the new MCP state knows none of them and refuses to start beside them, so have
  an ACCOUNTADMIN run those `DROP`s. The query before it prints a `REVOKE` for each grant
  `PROVISIONER` holds outside this project's database and warehouse, which an install in
  another database left and the MCP equally refuses.
[%- endif %]
[% if 'mcp' in components -%]
- The MCP signs in to Snowflake as `VECTEN_PROVISIONER`, which only an ACCOUNTADMIN can set up,
  in the database the first deploy creates. So the first deploy ships no host. Then run
  `vecten mcp provisioner-script --env <env>`, which stores the provisioner's key pair as
  `MCP_PROVISIONER_PRIVATE_KEY` and prints the SQL. Have an ACCOUNTADMIN run it, record it with
  `--confirm-run`, and `vecten github push`: the ship workflow then ships every host.
[% endif -%]
[% endif %][% endif %]
