## Promotion order

Without `[deploy] promotion` in `vecten.toml`, every merge to the default branch ships `prod`.
With an order such as `["dev", "prod"]`, a merge ships `dev` only, and `prod` receives exactly
the commit `dev` ran, when you promote it.

### Adopting an order

1. Workflows rendered before promotion existed ship `prod` on every push whatever the order
   says, so the CLI refuses to record an order under them, naming `vecten update`. Run
   `vecten update` then, review and commit.
2. Copy `deploy/envs/prod.tfvars` to `deploy/envs/dev.tfvars`, set `environment = "dev"` and
   set `domain` to the name `dev` serves under, such as `dev.[[ domain ]]`.
[%- if warehouse == 'snowflake' and 'platform' in components %]
   Set `snowflake_account` to `dev`'s own Snowflake account: one account serves one
   environment.
[%- endif %]
3. `vecten bootstrap --env dev`, then `vecten deploy --env dev`, as for `prod`.
4. `vecten answer deploy.promotion dev,prod` records the order; `vecten github push` commits
   it. From that push on, a merge ships `dev` and sets the commit status `vecten/ship/dev`.
5. `vecten promote --env prod` ships `prod` the commit `dev` shipped last. The first promotion
   needs one push shipped to `dev` by these workflows, because only they set the status.

`vecten next` walks the order: `dev` through its deploy, sign-in and first user, then `prod`.
`vecten promote --status` lists what each environment shipped last and whether it is behind
the one before it. `vecten answer deploy.promotion none` and a push return to shipping `prod`
on every merge.

### What a promoted run reads from where

`vecten promote --env prod` dispatches `deploy-platform.yml` on the default branch with
`environment: prod` and `commit: <sha>`, and `vecten deploy --env prod` dispatches
`terraform-apply.yml` the same way. Before any host or Terraform changes, the run refuses a
commit that is not on the default branch, and one whose `vecten/ship/dev` status is not
`success`. Then:

- From the promoted commit: the bundle (DAGs, connectors, dbt, `osi/`, `evals/`,
  `vecten.toml`), `deploy/envs/prod.tfvars`, the Terraform roots, and the `vecten` version its
  `[project] scaffold_version` names, so `prod` gets what `dev` ran, configuration included.
- From the default branch's head: the workflow file, and the facts the CLI records under
  `[environments.prod]` in `vecten.toml` (whether `prod` is bootstrapped and deployed, its
  cloud ids and keys), which describe `prod` as it is now. A fact the promoted commit's CLI
  does not know is named in a warning and left out.

A `prod`-only tfvars change therefore reaches `prod` on the next promotion, not on the merge
that carries it. `vecten promote --env prod --commit <sha>` ships an older commit `dev`
shipped, which is how `prod` rolls back. Required reviewers on the GitHub environment `prod`,
where the plan offers them, hold every promotion until someone approves it.
[% endif %]
[% if render_deploy and 'mcp' in components %]
## First sign-in to the MCP

After the first deploy, `vecten next` walks these steps; this is what each one does.

- **OIDC, the default.** Record the issuer and client id with
  `vecten answer mcp.oidc_issuer <url>` and `vecten answer mcp.oidc_client_id <id>`, and the
  first admin with `vecten answer mcp.admins <email>`. Register
  `https://mcp.[[ domain ]]/oidc/callback` as an allowed redirect for that client in the
  identity provider. If the provider issued a client secret, store it with
  `vecten secret set MCP_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET --env <env>`. Then
  `vecten answer oidc_registered.<env> yes`. The first login of an address in `[mcp] admins`
  makes that person an admin.
- **Password sign-in.** `vecten answer mcp.auth password` opts out of OIDC. Then
  `vecten mcp admin-link --env <env>` prints the set-password page and a one-time token that
  sets the first admin's password once, within 15 minutes; run it again if it lapses. It runs with your cloud credentials, so it also
  recovers an admin who lost their password.
- **Push and wait.** Each answer changes `vecten.toml`; `vecten github push` commits it and
  waits for the ship that carries it to the MCP.
- **Sign in on this machine.** `vecten login --env <env>` signs the admin in; `vecten user`,
  `group` and `grant` then act as that person. The session lasts up to seven days;
  `vecten logout --env <env>` ends it.
[% endif %]

## Upgrading this project

Install the desired Vecten release, keeping all four Vecten packages at that version, then
run `vecten update --json`. The installed CLI supplies the target release; the command does
not download software, restart the stack or deploy infrastructure. Commit the project first.
Keep `.copier-answers.yml` and `.copier-baseline.json` in Git: they record the original
answers and pristine generated files used to merge your edits with later releases.

A successful result lists changed paths. A conflict exits 4 and lists paths while leaving
all files unchanged. Back up your edited files, compare each with its pristine baseline and
the new template, reconcile overlapping edits and retry. Do not delete the baseline to force
an update. An internal project without a baseline must initialize it with its original CLI
release before installing a newer release.

[% if 'platform' in components %]
Run `vecten dbt-build-local --check` after the update. It tests the model conventions and
checks that the generated semantic datasets agree with the models. If model definitions
changed, run without `--check` first, review the regenerated datasets, then check again.
[% endif %]
Run `vecten mcp validate`, review and commit the changed files. The next local `vecten up`
uses the installed release's images. A deployed project ships content when changes reach its
default branch after the first deployment; infrastructure changes require a separate reviewed
plan and approved `vecten deploy --env <env>`.
